      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "CD163 is a scavenger receptor for the uptake of haptoglobin-hemoglobin (Hpt-Hb) complexes. The Hpt-Hb complexes are being formed in the plaque in response to intraplaque hemorrhage, a hallmark of atherosclerotic plaque instability. We therefore investigated whether soluble CD163 (sCD163) was elevated in patients with an acute coronary syndrome."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "All subjects presenting with chest pain suggestive of myocardial ischemia referred to either the emergency department or the coronary care unit were included in a prospective follow-up study. Plasma was collected and frozen at -80 degrees C until assayed. sCD163 was measured using a commercially available Elisa assay."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Of 526 included chest pain patients, the final diagnosis was non-cardiac chest pain in 244 (46%) patients, non-STEMI in 67 (13%), and STEMI in 215 (41%). The non-STEMI patients were older, used more medication, had undergone more often coronary interventions, but did not differ with respect to risk factors, except for a higher incidence in dyslipidemia. Unexpectedly, sCD163 did not differentiate between patients with non-STEMI or STEMI and the non-cardiac chest pain patients (2.09+/-0.76 versus 2.24+/-0.86)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Although ACS is characterized by intraplaque hemorrhage, the amount of intraplaque Hb release seems not to be substantial enough to result in a measurable difference in sCD163."
        }
      ]},
